What is GitLab?
=> GitLab is a web-based platform that helps developers manage, collaborate on, and deploy code.
GitLab is a comprehensive, web-based DevSecOps platform used by developers to plan, build, secure, and deploy software. 
It functions as a central hub where teams can host code repositories (like Git), collaborate on code reviews, automate testing and deployment (CI/CD), and track project progress.

Think of it this way:

Git = The version control system that tracks changes to your code.
GitLab = A platform built around Git that stores your repositories online and provides many additional tools for software development.
GitLab is designed as a single application that covers the entire software development lifecycle, eliminating the need to stitch together multiple third-party tools.
Its core features include:
Source Code Management (SCM):
        Like Git, GitLab allows you to create, clone, push, and pull repositories. 
        It provides version control to ensure that every change to your code is tracked and managed efficiently.
CI/CD Pipelines:
        GitLab offers industry-leading continuous integration and continuous delivery. 
        This means every time code is committed, it can be automatically built and tested to catch bugs early, then automatically deployed to production.
Project Management:
         It includes built-in issue tracking, Kanban boards, and Scrum boards to help teams organize tasks, bugs, and feature requests.
Security Scanning:
         GitLab seamlessly integrates security into the development process (DevSecOps) by automatically scanning code for vulnerabilities and leaked secrets with every push.


